1 Opening Esploro
*****************

‘Super+e’
     The Esploro window on the workspace you're on, or a new one
     there.  On Vikix this is the key for files; where Esploro
     isn't installed, it opens PCManFM instead.

‘Super+Alt+Shift+e’
     A new Esploro window, even where one is open.

‘Super+Alt+r’
     Reveals the file behind the window you're in: what an Emacs
     frame shows, the document a viewer has open, the file a
     terminal's program was started on, or the folder a terminal
     is in; selected, in its folder.

‘esploro FOLDER’
     From a shell, the launcher, or a program opening a folder:
     FOLDER in the Esploro window on your workspace, or a new
     one there.  ‘esploro --new FOLDER’ always opens a new
     window, and ‘esploro FILE’ opens the file's folder with it
     selected.

Show in folder
     A browser's "Show in folder" (Firefox, Chromium), and any
     program that asks for the file manager over D-Bus, opens
     Esploro at the file's folder, with the file selected, on
     your workspace.  On Vikix, folders open in Esploro too, and
     so do drives when you plug them in.

‘M-x esploro’
     From Emacs, once Esploro has been opened (or with ‘emacs/’
     on Emacs's ‘load-path’).  With a prefix argument, a new
     window.

   Esploro's window is a frame of the Emacs that is running,
which on Vikix is a daemon started at login, so it opens at
once.  The first time, the command loads the window's code into
that Emacs; nothing in your Emacs configuration is needed.

   To close it, use File, Close Esploro, ‘C-x C-c’, or close the
window (‘Super+q’ on Vikix).  Only that window closes: Emacs,
and its other frames, go on.


File: esploro.info,  Node: The window,  Next: Selecting and opening,  Prev: Opening Esploro,  Up: Top

2 The window
************

The list
     The folder's files, folders first, with their size and when
     they last changed.  The line at the top says where you are,
     how the list is sorted, and when hidden files are shown or
     a filter is on.

Places
     Down the left side: your home, the usual folders
     (Documents, Downloads, Pictures, Music, Videos, Projects,
     Dropbox, when they exist), drives plugged in (in
     ‘/run/media’), the bookmarks GTK programs keep
     (‘~/.config/gtk-3.0/bookmarks’: File ⇒ Bookmark This
     Folder, or ‘C-c b’, adds the folder you're in, and Remove
     Bookmark takes it out; PCManFM and the file dialogs share
     the list), the searches you kept (*note Searches::), and
     the Trash.  Click one to go there.  ‘F9’, or View, Places,
     hides them or shows them again.

The tool bar
     Back, Forward, Up, Home, Places, Two Panes, Preview, New
     Folder, Copy, Cut, Paste, Trash, Undo, Search, Refresh and
     Help.

The menus
     Esploro's frame shows only these; your other Emacs frames
     keep Emacs's own menus.

        • File: new window and folder, open, open with, Commands
          (what else suits the file: copy its path, compress,
          extract, shrink a picture, yours), properties, a
          terminal here, closing a project.
        • Edit: undo, repeat the last change and Recipes, copy,
          cut, paste and the rest, rename by a pattern, sort
          into folders by kind, the Trash, selecting.
        • View: sorting, hidden files, the filter, searching
          below, refresh, two panes, the preview, places.
        • Go: back, forward, up, home, a folder typed, your
          Searches, the Trash.
        • Help: this manual, and the keys.

     Right-click a file for what can be done with it;
     right-click the empty part of the list for the folder:
     paste, a new folder, searching, sorting.


