3 Selecting and opening
***********************

Click
     Selects the file: the one the next command acts on.

‘Ctrl’+click
     Adds the file to the selection, or takes it out.

‘Shift’+click
     Selects every file from the one selected last to the one
     clicked.

Double-click, or ‘RET’
     Opens it.  A folder opens in the same window.  A file that
     a window already has open goes to that window rather than
     opening a second time.  Text opens in Emacs, in one of its
     other frames.  Anything else opens in its usual program (as
     ‘~/.config/mimeapps.list’ says).

Right-click
     What can be done with the file under the mouse, or, on
     empty space, with the folder.

File, Open With
     Opens the selection with a program you name.

   Edit, Select All, Select None and Invert Selection work on
every file in the folder.  Dired's keys still mark: ‘m’ marks,
‘u’ unmarks, ‘U’ unmarks all.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Changing files,  Next: Recipes,  Prev: Selecting and opening,  Up: Top

4 Changing files
****************

Copy, Cut, Paste (‘M-w’, ‘C-w’, ‘C-y’)
     Copy or cut the selection, go to another folder, and paste.
     Pasting where the files already are makes copies beside
     them ("notes copy.txt").  Other file managers can paste
     what Esploro copied, and Esploro can paste what they
     copied.

Duplicate
     A copy of each selected file beside it.

Copy To, Move To
     Copies or moves the selection into a folder you type.

Rename (‘F2’)
     A new name for the file selected.

New Folder (‘+’)
     A folder in the one you're in.

Move to Trash (‘Delete’)
     Puts the selection in the Trash.  Nothing is deleted for
     good there.

Undo (‘C-/’)
     Puts back the last change Esploro made.  Undo again for the
     one before.  Undoing a copy puts the copy in the Trash,
     rather than deleting it.

   Every change is a plan, a short list of steps, checked whole
before anything happens: a file that would be overwritten, or a
folder that isn't there, stops it with nothing changed, and the
message says why.  Changes run in the background, so a long copy
doesn't stop Emacs.  Each one is kept in a journal
(‘~/.local/state/esploro/journal/’), which is what undo reads.

   A long copy (or a move to another disk) says how it goes at
the bottom of the window: the file, how much is done of how
much, and ‘C-c C-k’ to stop it (Edit ⇒ Stop Copying).  Stopped,
the file being copied is taken back, as if it had never started;
the files done before stay, and undo takes them back too.  A
move to another disk is a copy, then the original deleted:
stopped during the copy, the original is as it was; once the
delete has begun, it finishes, so a file is never lost between
the two.

   Dired's own keys (‘C’, ‘R’, ‘D’ and the rest) still work in
the list, but around the journal: what they do can't be undone
from Esploro.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Recipes,  Next: Drag and drop,  Prev: Changing files,  Up: Top

5 Recipes
*********

Having moved some files into a folder, copied them into one, or
put them in the Trash, select others and press ‘z’ (Edit ⇒
Repeat Last Change): the same is done to them.  A name already
taken in the folder gets a free one ("report 2.pdf"); a file
already there isn't moved again.

   Edit ⇒ Recipes ⇒ Save Last Change As... keeps it by name.
Recipes you kept are listed there, and under Recipes in the
right-click menu, to do on what's selected; Forget a Recipe...
is there too.  They're kept in ‘~/.config/esploro/recipes.lisp’.

   A recipe is a plan like any other: checked whole, done in the
background, undone with ‘C-/’.

5.1 Habits noticed
==================

Esploro notices what you keep doing.  When three or more like
files (pictures, PDFs, names sharing a word) have gone into one
folder, by two or more of your plans or your corrections to an
agent's, it says so once, at the bottom of the window: "Esploro
noticed: You've moved 3 PDFs and e-books named "invoice" from
~/Downloads into ~/Work/Invoices."

   Edit ⇒ Habits Noticed... lists them, each with:

Keep as Rule...
     A sentence for your rules for where files go
     (‘sorting.md’), which agents read: shown first, to say what
     you mean.
Keep as Recipe...
     "Move into that folder", by name, under Edit ⇒ Recipes.
Find More
     A search for more like them where they came from, to select
     and move the same way.
Not This
     Never offered again.

   Nothing is done on its own.

5.2 Rename by a pattern
=======================

Edit ⇒ Rename by Pattern... (also under Recipes) asks for the
names to change, then what to make of them.  In the first, ‘*’
stands for any run of characters (as few as will do) and ‘?’ for
any one, in any case; in the second, ‘#1’, ‘#2’... give back
what each took, ‘#n’ numbers the files in the order they're
listed (01, 02..., as wide as the count needs), and ‘##’ is a
‘#’.  So ‘IMG_*.jpg’ to ‘Holiday #n.jpg’ makes "Holiday 1.jpg",
"Holiday 2.jpg" and so on, and ‘* - *.mp3’ to ‘#2 - #1.mp3’
turns "Artist - Song.mp3" round.  Without ‘*’ or ‘?’, the first
is text to replace wherever a name holds it: a space to ‘_’.

   It takes the marked files, or with none marked everything
listed (the pattern picks its own); names that don't fit are
left as they are.

5.3 Sort into folders by kind
=============================

Edit ⇒ Sort into Folders by Kind... sends pictures to Images,
videos to Videos, then Audio, Documents (PDFs, EPUBs), Text
(code too) and Archives: each a folder beside its file, made
when it isn't there.  It too takes the marked files, or
everything listed; a search's files are each sorted in their own
folder.  Folders, files of no kind it names (a spreadsheet) and
files already in their folder stay where they are.  A name
already taken in a folder gets a free one ("notes 2.txt").

5.4 Their plans wait for you
============================

Both send each file its own way, so their plan isn't done at
once: it waits under the folder, as an agent's does (*note Plans
from agents::).  A rename shows each name before and after; a
sorting, how many go where, what stays and why, and which file
got another name.  Apply, Edit or Cancel; nothing has changed
until Apply, and ‘C-/’ puts it back after.  A rename that would
give two files one name, or a name that's already taken, isn't
offered at all: Esploro says which.

   Keep as Recipe... (‘k’) keeps it by name, under Recipes, to
do again, again for review.  Your own sorting is written in
‘~/.config/esploro/recipes.lisp’, each kind to a folder: a name
is beside the file, a whole path gathers them there; the first
kind a file is wins, ‘:file’ is anything else and ‘:folder’ a
folder:

     (:recipe "Media" (:sort-by-kind (:image "Pictures") (:video "Pictures/Video")
                                     (:pdf "/home/vid/Documents/Papers")))
     (:recipe "Holiday" (:rename-by "IMG_*.jpg" "Holiday #n.jpg"))


