6 Drag and drop
***************

In
     Drag files from another program onto the list: they're
     copied into the folder (moved, when the other program says
     so, often with ‘Shift’).  Like every change, it can be
     undone.  Files dropped on the folder they're already in are
     left alone.

Out
     Press on a file anywhere on its row and drag it to another
     program: a browser's upload, a mail, a terminal (which
     types its name).  It's copied; hold ‘Shift’ to move it.
     Selected files go together.

Between panes
     Drag from one pane to the other: the file is copied into
     that pane's folder.


File: esploro.info,  Node: The preview,  Next: Two panes,  Prev: Drag and drop,  Up: Top

7 The preview
*************

Down the right of the window, the file under the cursor:

A picture
     The picture, scaled to fit.
A PDF, a video
     The PDF's first page, or a frame of the video.  Esploro
     makes it once, in the background ("making a preview..."
     while it does), and keeps it in ‘~/.cache/esploro’, so the
     next look is at once.
Text, code
     Its first lines, in the colours its mode gives them.
A folder
     How many things are in it, and their names.
Anything else
     What it is, as ‘file’ says.
Several selected
     How many, their size together, and their names.

   Below each: its size, when it last changed, and the windows
that have it open.  The preview follows the cursor and the mouse
once they rest, so holding an arrow key down stays quick.  With
two panes, it shows the pane used last; each Esploro window has
its own.

   ‘F11’ (View, Preview, or the tool bar) hides it, or shows it
again.  Hidden stays hidden, in every Esploro window, until you
turn it on.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Two panes,  Next: Windows and workspaces,  Prev: The preview,  Up: Top

8 Two panes
***********

‘F3’ (View, Two Panes, or the tool bar) shows two folders side
by side.  The new pane starts at the same folder; each then has
its own folder, history, sort and hidden files.  ‘F3’ again goes
back to one.

Copy to Other Pane (‘C-c C-c’)
Move to Other Pane (‘F6’)
     The selection, into the other pane's folder.  Both are on
     the Edit menu and the right-click menu.

   The places and the tool bar act on the pane used last.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Windows and workspaces,  Next: Looking around,  Prev: Two panes,  Up: Top

9 Windows and workspaces
************************

9.1 Opening on another workspace
================================

Right-click a file (or File) ⇒ Open on Workspace lists your
workspaces, each with what it's about: the project its windows
are in ("3 ~/src/vikix"), "empty", or how many windows it has.
Pick one and you're there, with the file open: text in an Emacs
frame on that workspace (one already there, or a new one), a
folder in an Esploro window there, anything else in its usual
program.  ‘M-o’ then the workspace's number does the same from
the keyboard.

9.2 One Esploro per workspace
=============================

Each workspace can have its own Esploro window, at its own
folder.  A new one opens in the workspace's project: the folder
most of its windows are about (a terminal's folder, Emacs's
files, a viewer's document), taken up to its project, the
nearest folder with a ‘.git’ or a ‘log.md’; your home on an
empty workspace.  ‘Super+e’ goes to the one on the workspace
you're on, or opens one there; one on another workspace stays
where it is.  To have two on the same workspace, use
‘Super+Alt+Shift+e’, File, New Window (‘C-x 5 2’), or ‘esploro
--new’.

   Esploro knows which window is on which workspace by asking
StumpWM. Without StumpWM, ‘esploro FOLDER’ uses any Esploro
window.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Looking around,  Next: Searches,  Prev: Windows and workspaces,  Up: Top

10 Looking around
*****************

Sorting (View, or ‘s’)
     By name, size (largest first), time (newest first) or kind.
     The same again, or View, The Other Way Round, turns it
     round.  ‘s’ goes through them in turn.

Hidden files (‘.’)
     Shows or hides the files whose names start with a dot.

Thumbnails (‘T’)
     A small picture before each picture, PDF and video in the
     list, made in the background (a big folder fills in as it
     goes) and kept, so they're there at once the next time.
     Their size is ‘esploro-thumbnail-height’ (48 pixels).

Git
     In a folder inside a git repository, each file git has
     something to say about says it after its name: _modified_,
     _new_ (git doesn't follow it yet), _staged_ or _added_ (for
     the next commit), _conflict_; a folder with changes inside
     says so.  The line at the top has the branch, and what's
     waiting: "git: main, 2 to push".  Git is asked in the
     background each time the folder is shown;
     ‘esploro-git-status’ set to ‘nil’ turns it off.

Grid (‘G’)
     The folder as tiles, side by side: a picture of each
     picture, PDF and video, a folder or a page (with its type)
     for the rest, the name under it.  Everything works as in
     the list: click selects (the tile is outlined), Ctrl+click
     and Shift+click select more (shaded), a double click opens,
     right-click has the same menu, and copy, paste, rename, the
     Trash and undo act on the tiles selected.  The arrow keys
     go from tile to tile.  Each window remembers whether it's a
     grid; going into a folder keeps it.  The size is
     ‘esploro-grid-size’ (128 pixels).

Filter (‘/’)
     Shows only the names holding some text, in any case, until
     you refresh (‘F5’).

Search Below (‘M-s s’)
     Every file below this folder that a few words describe, as
     a list you can open, copy and drag from like any folder.
     Back returns.  *Note Searches::.

Going (‘M-Left’, ‘M-Right’, ‘M-Up’, ‘C-l’)
     Back, forward, the folder above, and a folder typed, with
     completion.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Searches,  Next: Archives,  Prev: Looking around,  Up: Top

11 Searches
***********

View ⇒ Search Below... (‘M-s s’, or right-click the folder)
looks below the folder you're in for the files a few words
describe, and shows them as if they were one folder: open them,
drag them, copy or move them as anywhere else.  Every word must
hold:

‘report’
     A name holding "report", in any case.
‘*.pdf’, ‘IMG_20??*’
     Names like it: ‘*’ any run of characters, ‘?’ any one.
‘kind:pdf’
     Of a kind: folder, file, image, video, audio, pdf, text,
     lisp, archive.
‘newer:7’, ‘older:30’
     Changed in the last 7 days, or not for 30.
‘larger:10M’, ‘smaller:1k’
     By size (k, M, G).
‘has:tender’
     A file holding "tender" inside it, in any case: text files
     (found by ‘ripgrep’, quick), and PDFs (their text taken out
     once with ‘pdftotext’ and kept, so the first search of many
     PDFs takes a while and the next ones don't).
‘-draft’
     Not: a word with ‘-’ in front.

   So ‘kind:pdf newer:30 -invoice’ is the PDFs of the last month
that aren't invoices, and ‘kind:pdf has:LME’ the PDFs that
mention LME. Hidden files and folders aren't looked in, nor what
a project's ‘.gitignore’ leaves out (when ‘fd’ is installed),
nor ‘node_modules’.  At most 1000 are shown.

   ‘F5’ looks again.  Go ⇒ Searches ⇒ Keep This Search... (or
right-click the list) keeps it by name: it's down the side under
Searches from then on, a click away, and always up to date.
They're kept in ‘~/.config/esploro/searches.lisp’, where a
search can also be written as an s-expression: ‘(:and (:kind
:pdf) (:or (:name "tax") (:name "receipt")))’.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Archives,  Next: The Trash,  Prev: Searches,  Up: Top

12 Archives
***********

Open a zip, a tarball (‘.tar.gz’, ‘.tgz’, ‘.tar.xz’,
‘.tar.zst’...), a 7z, a rar or an ISO the way you open a folder,
and you're inside it: the line at the top says "inside
~/Downloads/pack.zip (read-only)".  Look around, preview, open
files, copy them out or drag them to another program, as in any
folder.

   Nothing can be changed inside: copying in, renaming, the
Trash are refused, with what to do instead.  To change what's in
it, Commands ⇒ Extract Here, and work on the folder that makes.

   Up from its top goes back beside the archive.  Leaving it
closes it; Back goes in again.  It's opened with ‘archivemount’,
which Esploro's feature in Vikix installs; without it, Extract
Here still works.


File: esploro.info,  Node: The Trash,  Next: Where files are open,  Prev: Archives,  Up: Top

13 The Trash
************

Esploro uses the Trash other programs use (the freedesktop one,
in ‘~/.local/share/Trash’).  Go, The Trash (or Trash in the
places) shows what's in it.  There, Restore from the Trash puts
the selection back where it was; undo puts it in the Trash
again.  Empty the Trash deletes everything in it, for good,
after asking: that one can't be undone.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Where files are open,  Next: Closing a project,  Prev: The Trash,  Up: Top

14 Where files are open
***********************

Beside a file that a window has open, Esploro says which: "open
in Emacs on 2 (unsaved)", "open in mpv on 4".  It asks StumpWM
for the windows and their programs, ‘/proc’ for what those hold
open or were started on, and Emacs for its buffers.  Opening
such a file goes to its window instead of opening it again.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Closing a project,  Next: Plans from agents,  Prev: Where files are open,  Up: Top

15 Closing a project
********************

Done with a project for now?  File ⇒ Close Project..., in any
folder of it, lists what's open in it: the files not saved
first, each with ‘Save’ and ‘Close’; then the rest open in Emacs
(files, dired, shells working there), each with ‘Close’; then
the other windows that have something in it, a terminal in its
folder or a PDF open in a viewer, each with ‘Go’.

   ‘Save All’ saves every unsaved file.  ‘Close All’ closes
what's saved, and leaves what isn't, so nothing is lost by a
click: save it or close it on its own (closing asks first).
Other windows are for you to close.  ‘g’ looks again.

   A project is the nearest folder above with a ‘.git’ or a
‘log.md’ in it.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Plans from agents,  Next: Keys and mouse,  Prev: Closing a project,  Up: Top

16 Plans from agents
********************

An agent (Claude Code, through Vikix's ‘vikix mcp’) never moves,
copies, renames or deletes your files itself.  It proposes a
plan, which the core checks whole (one with a problem goes back
to the agent), and Esploro shows it to you, on your workspace:
why, and each step.  ‘Apply’ makes the changes, journaled, so
undo takes them back; ‘Cancel’ drops the plan, and nothing
changes.

   ‘Edit’ (or ‘e’) opens the plan as text, one step a line:

     (:move "/from" "/to")    (:copy "/from" "/to")
     (:rename "/path" "new name")
     (:mkdir "/path")         (:trash "/path")

   Change, add or delete lines.  Each save checks the plan
again, and the review shows it as it now is, marked as edited by
you, with what's wrong if anything is (a plan with a problem
isn't applied).  ‘C-c C-c’ saves and goes back to the review;
‘C-c C-k’ drops your unsaved changes.  Then ‘Apply’ as before.

   What you changed is a correction, and Esploro can teach it to
every agent.  After you apply a plan you edited, it says how
many of the agent's steps you changed and asks whether to keep
them as rules.  On yes, each comes as a sentence to make general
before it's kept: ""Dataroom LME.zip" goes in ~/Work, not in
~/Archives."  might become "A work zip goes in Work, not in
Archives."  Leave one empty to skip it.  Kept rules go at the
end of your rules for where files go,
‘~/.config/esploro/sorting.md’, under "Learnt from my
corrections", and every agent that proposes changes reads that
file first.  Nothing is written there without your yes; the file
is yours to change (Help ⇒ Your Sorting Rules).  Every
correction is also noted, as data, in
‘~/.local/state/esploro/corrections.lisp’.

   Agents, and shells, can read what you've selected: ‘esploro
selection’ prints the files selected in the Esploro window you
used last (or a dired buffer, if you used one since), one a
line; with nothing marked, the file the cursor is on.  So you
can select files, then ask an agent to "sort the ones I've
selected", or run ‘tar czf out.tgz $(esploro selection)’ in a
terminal.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Keys and mouse,  Next: The command line,  Prev: Plans from agents,  Up: Top

17 Keys and mouse
*****************

Mouse
----------------------------------------------------------------------
click                     select it
double-click              open it (a folder goes in)
Ctrl+click                add it to the selection, or take it out
Shift+click               select everything from the last one to
                          here
right-click               what can be done with it, or with the
                          folder
drag a row out            give the file to another program
drop files in             copy them here
Keys
----------------------------------------------------------------------
RET                       open
M-Left, M-Right           back, forward
M-Up or ^                 the folder above
