19 Writing commands
*******************

Commands are on Esploro's menus (File ⇒ Commands, and the
right-click menu), and anywhere in Emacs on a file name: with
embark, ‘C-.’ then ‘,’ (comma) offers Esploro's commands for the
file (in a minibuffer, dired, or at point), and ‘J’ shows it in
Esploro.  ‘M-x esploro-file-commands’ asks for a file.  For that
before Esploro is first opened, your Emacs loads its little
loader at the start:

     (load "~/.local/opt/esploro/emacs/esploro-loaddefs" t t)

   On Vikix, ‘Super+Alt+x’ offers them for the file behind any
window, in rofi.

   The core defines commands once, for the kinds of file they're
for:

     (esploro:define-file-command shrink ((path :image) :changes t)
       "Plan a smaller copy beside it."
       (list (list :copy path (esploro::free-name path " small"))))

   Kinds: ‘:folder’, ‘:image’, ‘:video’, ‘:audio’, ‘:pdf’,
‘:text’, ‘:lisp’, ‘:archive’, ‘:file’ (anything but a folder)
and ‘t’ (anything).  A command with ‘:changes t’ returns plan
steps instead of touching files, so it is checked, journaled and
undoable like the rest.  Offering these in the window's menus is
the next step.


